                <h2>Why Can’t Agency Account Opening Rely Only on Spreadsheets and Chat Groups?</h2>
<p>Many agency teams initially manage client onboarding through spreadsheets, chat messages, and email. This works reasonably well for a small number of clients, but the process becomes difficult as volume grows. Sales may consider an account “completed” while the delivery team discovers that the platform still has restrictions. Meanwhile, clients may have paid for funding without the balance appearing in the advertising account.</p>
<p>The core issue is not necessarily a lack of effort. It is the absence of a unified operational view. Agency account opening involves client requirements, business information, platform reviews, account limits, funding, exception handling, and final delivery. A systemized dashboard turns fragmented updates into clear, actionable statuses.</p>
<h2>Five Questions Clients Often Ask</h2>
<h3>1. “Why can’t you confirm the result immediately after we submit the documents?”</h3>
<p>Submission is only the beginning of the workflow; it does not mean the platform has finished reviewing the application. A dashboard should distinguish between pending submission, under review, additional information required, approved, rejected, pending funding, and delivered. It should also record the owner and latest update time for each stage.</p>
<p>If a case remains under review beyond the expected processing time, the responsible team should investigate proactively rather than asking the client to chase an update. Clear progress is more useful than a generic “we’re handling it.”</p>
<h3>2. “Can we start advertising as soon as the account is approved?”</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Being able to log in, being eligible to advertise, having sufficient balance, and having complete permissions are four different conditions. Before delivery, the agency should verify the account type, advertising permissions, balance, payment method, billing settings, and any risk-related restrictions.</p>
<p>For clients that need to launch immediately, a basic backend check and a test campaign should be included in the delivery process. This reduces avoidable issues such as failed payments or campaigns being assigned to the wrong business entity.</p>
<h3>3. “Why do different clients experience different processing times?”</h3>
<p>Differences may come from document quality, jurisdiction, platform review queues, intended account use, or previous risk history. The dashboard should record more than success or failure. It should classify the reason, such as incomplete documents, identity verification failure, incompatible payment method, inconsistent business information, or additional review.</p>
<p>Without structured exception categories, it is impossible to determine whether the next step is to request documents, change the application approach, or warn the client about a potential delay. “Review failed” is not an adequate root-cause label.</p>
<h3>4. “Can account-opening progress and funding be managed in the same place?”</h3>
<p>Yes, but they should be tracked separately. Opening progress answers when the account will become available, while funding balance answers when it can run campaigns. The dashboard should show the funding amount, processing status, arrival time, and available balance, with alerts for low balances.</p>
<p>For clients managing multiple advertising accounts, teams should distinguish the platform-level balance from the balance of each individual account. Finance and operations should also be able to trace the order number, paying entity, funded entity, and receiving advertising account.</p>
<h3>5. “How can the agency prove that delivery is complete?”</h3>
<p>Delivery should be based on a standard checklist rather than verbal confirmation. It should cover business information verification, account status, permissions, funding arrival, test campaign, account assignment, risk notifications, and the assigned service owner.</p>
<p>A team may mark a case as delivered only after these fields are complete. This reduces omissions and makes it easier to investigate complaints or internal quality reviews.</p>
<h2>What Should an Agency Account-Opening Dashboard Show?</h2>
<p>The dashboard does not need complex charts. It should prioritize operational information:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Client and account status:</strong> Pending documents, under review, action required, approved, pending funding, and delivered.</li>
<li><strong>Key milestones:</strong> Submission, first feedback, document resubmission, review result, and delivery time.</li>
<li><strong>Funding and balances:</strong> Pending deposits, credited amounts, account balances, and low-balance alerts.</li>
<li><strong>Exception tasks:</strong> Missing documents, overdue reviews, failed payments, permission issues, owners, and deadlines.</li>
<li><strong>Delivery checklist:</strong> Track verified actions instead of using a vague “completed” status.</li>
</ul>
<h2>A Dashboard Does Not Replace People; It Clarifies Accountability</h2>
<p>A systemized dashboard addresses three practical needs: clients can see the next step, internal owners can see what requires action, and finance and operations can trace the relationship between payments, accounts, and delivery. The system does not need to be complex from day one. Standardized fields, statuses, and reminders already reduce repetitive communication and missed follow-ups.</p>
<p>The professionalism of an agency account-opening service should not be judged only by the number of accounts opened. It should also be judged by whether exceptions are detected early, funding is matched correctly, and delivery can be verified. For agencies, the dashboard is not merely an administrative tool; it is the operational infrastructure connecting client trust with actual advertising performance.</p>
